  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO \nCOUNTIES...\n\nA cold frontal band will continue to push through the county warning \narea this afternoon, dissipating by early evening. Areas of heavy \nrain associated with this system have have been observed over the LA \nbasin. Hourly rain rates have generally been between 0.40-0.60 \ninches per hour with rain totals nearing one inch in some spots over \nthe coastal slopes and near the LA County line. The cold front will \ncontinue to weaken, pushing south and east through the afternoon, \npushing through the San Diego metro around 2-5 PM.  Hourly rain \nrates will lower near one half inch across this area at times. Snow \nlevels around 7,000 feet early this afternoon will continue to drop \nnear 6,000 feet by the late afternoon hours. As the weather system \nmoves out of the area, light snow may fall in places like Big Bear \nLake near one half inch or so in total. Isolated light showers may \nlinger into parts of the San Diego metro on Friday as the system \nmoves out with clearing skies.\n\nCooler weather will stick around Friday into the weekend as another \nlow pressure system moves closer to the Pacific Northwest. Friday \nwill be mostly sunny but highs will be 5 to almost 15 degrees below \nnormal with temperatures only topping out in the 30s and 40s across \nthe mountains, only in the 50s in the high desert. As the low moves \ncloser to the Pacific Northwest, a weak shortwave moves into \nNorthern California. This will bring cloudier conditions to our area \nwith continued cooler weather. A portion of this shortwave may drift \nfar enough south and even become a closed low over the desert \nsouthwest. This will bring the chance for ever so light \nshowers/drizzle closer to the coast by Sunday night into Monday \nmorning. If anything falls, it will be light in nature.\n\nModels are in fair agreement about another large area of low \npressure moving over the NE Pac by early next week. There still \nremains a lot of uncertainty on where this system goes and what parts \nof it make it far enough to impact Southern California. Some \npossible scenarios as we head toward the Thanksgiving holiday \ninclude: the system staying to the north as high pressure to the \nsouth expands over our region. This would bring dry and warmer \nweather. The GFS handles this a bit differently, where the system \nsplits in two, and a potential trough moves closer to our region \nwhich would bring potentially wetter weather by around next \nWednesday. We are still close to a week away form this, so please \nkeep updated with the forecast.",
